            "location": "Curve expansion and tangent condition",
            "claim": "The order-three hypothesis implies T=0.",
            "verification": "The cubic expands to X^3+VX^2+UzX+z^2, and its derivative at X=1 is 3+2V+Uz. Since R is nonzero and 2S=-S, the duplication formula requires lambda^2=V+3. Substituting R^2=1+V+Uz+z^2 gives the stated equation -T=0."
          },
          {
            "location": "Square identity and integer specialization",
            "claim": "The same condition implies 1+z^2 is a rational square; if two seed entries are integers, then z is an integer.",
            "verification": "The displayed identity reduces to (2z^2+Uz-1)^2=4(1+z^2)R^2. With R nonzero rational, this makes 1+z^2 a rational square. Substituting U=A+z/B and V=B+Az/B into T=0 yields the stated monic integer quartic; the rational-root theorem applies to rational z."
          },
          {
            "location": "Scope",
            "claim": "The contradiction proves at most one integer among the specified order-three seed triple.",
            "verification": "For nonzero integer z, z^2<z^2+1<(|z|+1)^2, so z^2+1 cannot be an integer square. This excludes two integers only in the triple satisfying the specified canonical order-three hypothesis; it does not rule out other coordinate placements or resolve the sextuple problem."
          }
