            "location": "Opposite-sign integer anchors",
            "claim": "Such anchors must be {-1,1}.",
            "verification": "Their product is an integer at most -1, while its sum with 1 must be nonnegative. Thus the product is -1. For any other element x, the anchor conditions imply -1<=x<=1; distinctness and nonzero membership exclude all integer values in that interval."
          },
          {
            "location": "Common-neighbor parametrization",
            "claim": "Every nonzero rational common neighbor is f(t)=4t(1-t^2)/(1+t^2)^2 for rational t.",
            "verification": "Writing p^2=1+x and q^2=1-x gives p^2+q^2=2. For x nonzero, p!=1, so the line parameter t=(q-1)/(p-1) is finite; substitution and solving the resulting quadratic relation gives the stated p,q and f(t). Conversely, the displayed formulas give p^2+q^2=2 and x=f(t), proving both required square conditions."
          },
          {
            "location": "Symmetric sextuple criterion",
            "claim": "{1,-1,u,-u,v,-v} is a D(1) sextuple exactly under the stated C-membership, nonzero, and distinctness conditions.",
            "verification": "The conditions provide the anchor pairs and cross pairs; the opposite pairs use 1-u^2=(1+u)(1-u) and its v analogue. Conversely, each required pair forces the listed conditions. The nonzero and u!=±v requirements are exactly the remaining distinctness constraints."
          }
