            "claim": "For x=u/v in lowest terms, v=d_a r^2=d_b s^2 and au+v=d_a A^2, bu+v=d_b B^2.",
            "verification": "The gcd identities reduce the denominators of ax+1 and bx+1 to v/d_a and v/d_b. A reduced rational square has square numerator and denominator, including the zero-numerator case."
          },
          {
            "location": "Deep-denominator residue argument",
            "claim": "If e>max(alpha,beta), the residue a_0/b_0 modulo p is a nonzero square.",
            "verification": "Under this strict inequality, u, a_0, b_0, d_{a,0}, and d_{b,0} are p-adic units. Reducing the two numerator-square equations modulo p and taking their ratio shows (a_0/b_0)(d_{b,0}/d_{a,0}) is a square. The denominator-square equation makes d_{b,0}/d_{a,0} a square unit modulo p, yielding the claimed nonzero square residue."
          },
          {
            "location": "Scope",
            "claim": "The result is a local necessary condition, not an all-anchor obstruction.",
            "verification": "It constrains denominator valuations only when the strict depth condition holds, and rules them out when the specified residue is a nonresidue. It does not prove that such a prime exists for every anchor pair or establish the proposed four-neighbor obstruction."
